# Possibly a Hero — Canonical Manifest

Routing table for the canonical repo. Read first, every session, every
agent. This file states what documents exist, whether they are
authoritative, and where to fetch them. It does not state what they
contain — for that, fetch the document.

---

## How to use

- Fetch this manifest before any work. Then fetch the documents the work
  requires.
- Authority order: LOCKED > DRAFT. Deprecated documents have none.
- The manifest routes; it does not summarize. Never work from a Note in
  this file — fetch the document and read the source. A summary kept
  apart from its source drifts; this file refuses to hold one.
- No "last updated" date, by design. Git owns history; a hand-typed date
  is a liar. Run `git log` if you need it.

---

## Fetch protocol — non-negotiable

- Use bash_tool + curl with a cache-buster:
  `curl -sL "<RAW_URL>?cb=$(date +%s)"`. Never web_fetch — it caches
  silently and makes you wrong without erroring.
- Verify currency: check the fetched tail or final entry against known
  project state before trusting it.
- Stale despite the cache-buster → stop, request a manual paste. Manual
  paste is authoritative.
- Fetch fails → stop and report plainly. Do not work around it. Never
  fall back to memory.

---

## Status vocabulary

- LOCKED — non-negotiable; constrains everything below it.
- DRAFT — directionally committed; still refinable.
- NOTED — captured; not yet a formal decision.
- CANONICAL SOURCE — origin record of mixed-status entries; each entry
  governed by its own status.
- ACTIVE — operational document, current and in use.
- DEPRECATED — superseded; historical only, no authority.
- REFERENCE — supporting material; not subject to pipeline.

